{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzC3cPtCsMY24oyvsXhU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It's a real feature flag, but it looks unnecessary for the tests.\n\n**What it does.** `kiln/timeparse.py:3` says that `KILN_LEGACY_TZ=1` makes `parse()` read naive timestamps (no UTC offset) as UTC, which is how kiln behaved before 2.0. Without it, naive timestamps are read as local time. The code at `kiln/timeparse.py:12` does the same.\n\n**Whether the tests need it.** I ran `python3 -m unittest` without the variable and all 3 tests pass. The only naive-timestamp test (`tests/test_timeparse.py:10`) checks `.hour == 8`. That holds either way, because attaching UTC doesn't change the hour. So the flag has no effect on the test results. It was added in the initial setup commit (`d274cef`), and nothing records why it's in step 4.\n\nI think you're right that it's a leftover, but I can't be certain. Someone may have wanted tests to run in legacy mode on purpose, for example to match a deployment. If you want it gone, I'd drop it from step 4 as a separate commit. I haven't changed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":444,"cache_read_input_tokens":22499,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":444},"output_tokens":376,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-53>","timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:08:38.947Z","request_id":"req_011CgzC3bwbvUUwzNmEWMtTN"}
